1: # P3F.29. All logarithmic orders in the coprime OS49 sector
2: 
3: **Proved.** Keep the actual component-supported construction of
4: P3F.26. Let \(m=3k\), with \(k>0\), and choose the logarithmic
5: vector
6: \[
7:  \ell=n(1,-1,0,0)^t+d(0,0,0,1)^t,
8:  \qquad \gcd(n,m)=1.                                  \tag{P3F.29.1}
9: \]
10: The root-chart action of order \(m\), with the same order-three
11: linear part, is free. The resulting smooth compact complex
12: threefold has
13: \[
14:  \pi_1(X)=\mathbb Z/|n|,
15:  \qquad H_i(X;\mathbb Z)=\mathbb Z/|n|
16:        \quad(1\leq i\leq4),
17:  \qquad H_5(X;\mathbb Z)=0,
18:  \qquad H_0=H_6=\mathbb Z.                            \tag{P3F.29.2}
19: \]
20: Consequently every \(n=1\) or \(n=-1\) member is the standard
21: smooth six-sphere, for every \(k\) and every delta twist \(d\).
22: This statement determines topology in its coprime sector.
23: P3F.31--32 construct and compute the actual positive-fan page and
24: return; P3K.17 proves equality with the negative-fan return, so
25: both sphere sectors have index zero. P3F.34 supplies the additional
26: torsion factor in the more general noncoprime free-cap sector.
27: 
28: **Proof: the actual finite cap.** Write \(A=\widehat T_A\) for
29: the homology matrix in P3F.26. It fixes \(\ell\), and \(A^3=I\).
30: On the \(m\)-th-root chart use the affine action
31: \[
32:  \alpha(z)=Az+\ell/m.
33: \]
34: Its \(m\)-th power is translation by the integral vector \(\ell\).
35: The invariant integral covector \(\psi\) evaluates to \(n\) on
36: \(\ell\). Thus the nonidentity powers have nonintegral gamma
37: displacements \(jn/m\), for \(0<j<m\). They have no fixed
38: points. The punctured gluing is the logarithmic translation already
39: displayed in P3F.26, now with \(t=s^m\). Its inverse and branch
40: independence are the same explicit formulas. This constructs the
41: specified smooth cap and its attachment to the unchanged ordinary,
42: type \(II\), and component cusp pieces.
43: 
44: The invariant first-cohomology lattice upstairs has basis
45: \[
46:  \psi,\qquad h=-w+3\delta.
47: \]
48: The integer map \(\Lambda\to\mathbb Z^2\) given by these two
49: covectors is onto and has kernel \((A-I)\Lambda\): the matrix
50: \(A-I\) has primitive image of rank two. After adjoining the
51: affine generator, the abelianized cap group therefore has the
52: presentation
53: \[
54:  \mathbb Z^3/\langle(n,3d,-m)\rangle.
55: \]
56: It is free of rank two because \(\gcd(n,m)=1\). Poincare duality
57: and the universal coefficient theorem show that the cap surface's
58: integral cohomology is torsion-free, with unimodular degree-two
59: intersection form.
60: 
61: Its integral first-cohomology pullback is exactly
62: \[
63:  L_1=\{a\psi+b h:na+3db\equiv0\pmod m\}.
64: \]
65: If \(\bar n n\equiv1\pmod m\), a basis is
66: \(m\psi,\ h-3d\bar n\psi\). In particular the global
67: invariant \(\psi\) has specialization index \(m\).
68: 
69: **Proof: all specialization lattices.** Keep the degree-two
70: invariants of P3F.26,
71: \[
72:  u_2=\psi h=-\psi w+3\psi\delta,
73:  \qquad v_2=(\psi+u)w=\psi w+uw,
74:  \qquad\omega=u_2+v_2.
75: \]
76: The subgroup generated by \(\alpha^3\) is the pure translation
77: subgroup of order \(k\); it enlarges the original torus lattice
78: by \(\ell/k\). An integral alternating form descends to that
79: torus precisely when contraction by \(\ell\) is divisible by
80: \(k\). Here the contractions are exactly
81: \[
82:  \iota_\ell u_2=nh-3d\psi,
83:  \qquad \iota_\ell v_2=0.
84: \]
85: Since \(n\) is invertible modulo \(k\), the invariant
86: degree-two forms on the intermediate torus have basis
87: \(ku_2,v_2\). Their intersection matrix, computed using that
88: torus's fundamental class, is \(3U\): the original pairing
89: \(u_2v_2=3\) is multiplied by \(k\) in the form and divided
90: by \(k\) by the covering degree.
91: 
92: The remaining covering has degree three. Its downstairs
93: intersection lattice is unimodular, so its degree-two pullback
94: has determinant nine. The full invariant lattice \(3U\)
95: already has determinant of absolute value nine. The index formula
96: for a sublattice therefore forces this pullback to be the whole
97: invariant lattice. In the original torus the precise degree-two
98: specialization lattice is consequently
99: \[
100:  L_2=\mathbb Z(ku_2)\oplus\mathbb Z v_2.
101: \]
102: Thus \(\omega\) has index \(k\), and its image generates
103: the local specialization cokernel \(\mathbb Z/k\).
104: 
105: For degree three the full invariant lattice has basis
106: \[
107:  \phi=\psi uw,
108:  \chi=(\psi+u)w\delta.
109: \]
110: The nonzero pairings with \(\psi,h\), in the original positive
111: volume \(\nu=\psi uw\delta\), are
112: \(\psi\chi=\nu\) and \(h\phi=-3\nu\).
113: Perfect pairing downstairs and the covering degree \(m\)
114: therefore identify its pullback lattice exactly as
115: \[
116:  L_3=\{a\phi+b\chi:a+d\bar n b\equiv0\pmod k\}.
117: \]
118: Indeed pairing with \(m\psi\) imposes no condition, while
119: pairing with \(h-3d\bar n\psi\) requires
120: \(m\mid3(a+d\bar n b)\). The class \(\phi\) has index
121: \(k\) and generates the cokernel. In degree four the covering
122: degree gives index \(m\). We have proved the four global
123: specialization indices
124: \[
125:                         (m,k,k,m).                  \tag{P3F.29.3}
126: \]
127: The global invariant reaches each full local cokernel. The
128: skyscraper exact sequence therefore creates no extra first
129: cohomology of the direct-image sheaves. Their parabolic complexes
130: and their free coinvariant groups are exactly the executed ones
131: in P3F.26; the local system and the nonfinite fillings have not
132: changed.
133: 
134: **Proof: fundamental group and every differential.** The two
135: nonfinite fillings give the same primitive relation lattice as
136: P3F.26, with quotient generated by gamma. The remaining base
137: meridian dies by the base relation. Its \(m\)-th power supplies
138: the relation \(\ell=0\), which becomes \(n\gamma=0\), since
139: the delta generator already dies in the nonfinite quotient. These
140: are all the boundary generators, so van Kampen gives the asserted
141: fundamental group.
142: 
143: The inversion and coarse linear-cap comparison of P3F.26 applies
144: without change. The actual translation prism now gives
145: \[
146:  d_2(a)=\frac{H_B}{m}[\iota_\ell a].
147: \]
148: In the four coinvariant targets the contractions of
149: \(\psi,\omega,\phi,\nu\) are respectively
150: \[
151:  n,\quad3n[\delta],\quad3n\eta,\quad n[uw\delta].
152: \]
153: Every term involving \(d\) maps to zero. Multiplication by the
154: four source indices \((m,k,k,m)\), followed by division by
155: \(m=3k\), gives four differentials equal to multiplication
156: by \(n\). The integral targets and sources are free, so the
157: rational prism calculation determines these integral maps
158: uniquely. There are no other differentials. It follows that
159: \(H^2,H^3,H^4,H^5\) are \(\mathbb Z/|n|\), while
160: \(H^1=0\) and \(H^0=H^6=\mathbb Z\). The universal
161: coefficient theorem and Poincare duality give (P3F.29.2), without
162: extension ambiguity. For \(|n|=1\), simple connectivity and
163: these integral groups imply a homotopy sphere, and
164: \(\Theta_6=0\) gives the standard smooth sphere.
165: 
166: The symbolic contractions, pairings, invariant bases and
167: presentation projection are executed in
168: `code/part3/os49_all_orders.py`; its finite sample checks are
169: additional checks, not a replacement for the arbitrary-integer
170: lattice argument above. **End of proof.**
